WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.567
BUZZ HAYS: Hi, my
name is Buzz Hays.

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:09.900
I'm the Global Lead of Media
and Entertainment Solutions

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.050
for Google Cloud.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:13.140
And we have a very
special guest here today,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.840
Dan Lin, who is the Vice
President of Infrastructure

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.970
Services at Viacom CBS.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:21.450
We're here today to talk about
content migration strategies

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.110
to cloud.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.330
Now, as you all
can imagine we're

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:28.300
experiencing some fairly
challenging market conditions

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.200
at the moment.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.300
So a lot of media
companies are starting

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.110
to look into their archives
as a way of finding material

00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:38.000
to monetize while
we're on a hold

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.600
pattern for other
productions to be launched.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.900
So we have a number
of companies that

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.790
are looking into
their archives to find

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:49.690
content to license other
people to stream on their OTT

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:50.800
platforms.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.230
And because of these
delayed content pipelines

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.000
for new productions,
there's a lot

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.750
of interest in finding
gold in these archives.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:03.130
So people are looking back into
media archives of all kinds,

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.620
and that extends back
beyond digital media.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.412
It actually extends
back into analog media,

00:01:07.412 --> 00:01:09.870
which is something that we'll
talk a little bit about today

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:10.692
too.

00:01:10.692 --> 00:01:12.400
Our definition of
media and entertainment

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.840
is everything, whether it's
film, video, audiotape,

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:19.780
photographs, documents,
they all qualify

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:23.600
and they're all finding new
relevance in this day and age.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.690
So we find that with a
number of sports leagues,

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:28.990
there's a lot of
interest in trying

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:31.030
to leverage older content.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.510
People love to watch
highlights of older games,

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.730
so we're finding that across
all sports, which is fantastic.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:39.222
We're also seeing that there
are a number of new streaming

00:01:39.222 --> 00:01:41.680
entities that need more content
to put onto their channels,

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.830
and legacy content is
one of the backbones

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.330
of some of the bigger studios
like Disney Plus, for example.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.053
They have a very
extensive archive,

00:01:48.053 --> 00:01:50.470
and they've just started to
scratch the surface, actually.

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:53.740
So digging into their
archives and finding ways

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:59.110
to monetize these older titles,
it couldn't be better timing.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.930
Same with Apple and other
folks, like Netflix, they're

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:05.170
really looking for ways
to leverage older content

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:07.640
as we wait for newer
things to be produced.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:12.190
Now, the traditional methods
of digitization and storage

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:16.300
are fairly laborious
and somewhat costly.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.690
They're very labor-intensive.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.760
So what we find
is that in cloud,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.630
there are a number of ways
that we can help people migrate

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:24.790
their collections
to cloud, and that

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.060
includes media in any form.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.510
As I mentioned, there's a lot
of analog media out there,

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.880
which is difficult to
monetize, obviously,

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.760
until it's in digital form,
until it's cleaned up,

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.150
until it's made pristine, and
made available to the public.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.180
So we want to make
sure that we have

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.490
enough tools available
for the folks in cloud

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:44.590
to be able to ingest
this material,

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:46.990
to convert it to the
proper digital formats,

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.620
and then they can start to
bring this content to market.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.440
As you can see here,
42% of media CIOs

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.930
are struggling with managing
their content in general.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.360
And this is pre the
current conditions

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:01.930
of COVID and the environment
within which we're not

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.250
producing new content.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.320
There's a lot of archive
content out there

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.160
that hasn't been monetized.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:10.092
The analogy is like an
art museum, where over 80%

00:03:10.092 --> 00:03:11.800
of the collection is
typically in storage

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.120
and the public never sees it.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:15.430
Some of these media archives
are quite substantial,

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:19.367
and a lot of their material
sits behind firewalls

00:03:19.367 --> 00:03:20.450
where people can't see it.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.210
So now is an opportunity to
start bringing some of that

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:23.170
forward.

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:26.020
Now, there's some legacy
digitization capabilities

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.620
out there in the world.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.680
And we need those in
order to get material

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.280
off these analog forms, like
videotape and film and audio,

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.440
for example.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.990
Especially with film, film
is a difficult process

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:41.920
because it can be done in
slightly less than real time.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:44.260
And videotape is
limited by the fact

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:46.430
that we can only
digitize in real time.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.730
So for the 100 million
hours of videotape

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.010
that might be out there
for any given entity that

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.980
wants to digitize things, it's
going to take 100 million hours

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:54.890
to digitize that.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.130
So we have some significant
challenges ahead,

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.740
but the idea is to bring
all of this material

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:03.370
into cloud so that it can be
then monetized and leveraged

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:07.070
to increase the value of these
archives as we go forward.

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:09.400
So as an example, we
did a project recently

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.950
with the New York Times
where we helped them

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:13.540
with their photo archive.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.480
They have one of the largest
photo archives in the world,

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:20.110
and the archive goes back to
the beginning of photography.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:22.870
And so we used a number
of our AI and ML tools

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.170
to help the New York
Times to find information

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:29.150
about the photographs, not
only the visual information,

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:31.540
but also by reading the
information that was either

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.940
handwritten or typed on
the back of the photos

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.370
about the uses,
the locations, who

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:37.840
might be in the photographs.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.300
And then created a
database within which

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:43.100
they can search this
material at lightning speed.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.360
So in the past, it might
take a few months for someone

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.468
to put together a
story, for example,

00:04:49.468 --> 00:04:51.010
where they wanted
to do a photo essay

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:54.370
or photojournalistic exploration
of a specific subject.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.020
They would have to
hire an archivist

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.600
to go in through
the files manually,

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.090
to find these photographs, to
find the individuals they're

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.670
talking about, which may
not be tagged appropriately,

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.080
and then send those out.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:08.560
So we've gone now from
the manual process

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.110
to a much more automated process
with a very robust database,

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:13.510
which allows people
to really cut

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:15.820
the amount of time it takes
to get these stories out

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:16.880
to the public.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:19.390
So we're seeing significant
increases in the speed

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:22.150
from many, many months,
now to a matter of days

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.030
to be able to
leverage the content.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:27.720
So this is just one example.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.540
Now, archive migration
within Google Cloud,

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:33.940
we look at it, basically,
as three distinct phases.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:36.270
The first phase is the
digitization phase.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.550
We have to get this
material into cloud.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.220
Now, some of it may be
digital born already,

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:43.460
especially modern motion
pictures and television

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:45.600
where they're all shot
natively digitally.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.287
In fact, most all of the
workflow itself is digital.

00:05:48.287 --> 00:05:49.870
There are still a
few that shoot film,

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:52.650
but primarily it's going
to be a digital workflow.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.540
Those files are very
easy to move to cloud,

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.300
but once you start talking
about other media types,

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.610
then the idea of
ingesting to cloud

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:01.883
becomes a bit more
labor-intensive.

00:06:01.883 --> 00:06:04.425
There are more steps involved,
and we'll outline some of that

00:06:04.425 --> 00:06:08.490
that we're helping with
in this presentation.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:10.500
There's also a quality
control aspect to this.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.030
There's a lot of
media out there.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:14.130
And quality control
really requires human eyes

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.400
to really make some decisions.

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.490
So we're helping
those human eyes

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:19.620
by leveraging some
of the AI tools

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.920
we have so that people
can have, basically,

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:23.880
a checklist to go by.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.940
So when AI finds something
that might be suspect

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:29.820
or some quality control
software find something suspect,

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.440
we can put a list
together and put it

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:32.760
in front of the people
who need to look

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:33.930
at this with their
own eyes and make

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.230
those decisions for themselves.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:36.915
So it's a much
simpler process rather

00:06:36.915 --> 00:06:38.790
than having people look
at every single frame

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.770
of every piece of content.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:44.860
On the generate side, once we
get this material into cloud,

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:47.770
whether it's digitally born
or started as an analog video

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:49.590
tape, and then we digitize.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.840
We create proxies for
that, which are very

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.010
important for a few reasons.

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.770
Primarily because we need
a reference copy, something

00:06:55.770 --> 00:07:00.360
that we can look at that we
can use to search for material.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.090
So the core of many media
asset management systems

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:06.330
is a proxy file, and
oftentimes the metadata

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:08.760
that goes along with
that is the way people

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.720
are able to search
these databases.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.220
So we, during this phase,
leverage all of the AI and ML

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:17.400
tools we have to be able
to provide intelligence

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.160
to this data through what's
found in the picture,

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:21.690
through speech to text.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:23.340
We can do sentiment
detection, we

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.250
can do celebrity recognition, we
have a number of different ways

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.590
where we can build a very
robust database, which

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:32.040
makes it very easy to search
these mountains of information

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.512
and make intelligent
decisions on which content

00:07:34.512 --> 00:07:36.720
we might want to put out
there on streaming channels,

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:38.580
for example.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.630
Indexing the media is
extremely important,

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:43.047
and we have a tool that we'll
talk a little bit about here

00:07:43.047 --> 00:07:46.110
that's called video search,
which is a new tool that allows

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.720
us to apply all of the
machine learning and AI tools

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:51.120
that we have as a
single command line.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.880
It will then build a database
of all of this metadata,

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:55.890
and then we can
surface that data

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:58.950
through a search API that could
be leveraged within whatever

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:00.558
application people want to use.

00:08:00.558 --> 00:08:02.100
So say, for example,
you're an editor

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.550
and you work in Adobe Premiere
or in Avid or in Black Magic

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:06.360
Resolve.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.790
You can now leverage
the search capability

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:10.920
within the application
itself, which

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:13.950
means you no longer have to
rely on going to a media asset

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:17.220
management system, finding
the clips you're looking for,

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.800
exporting those clips,
then reimporting

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.350
those clips into your editing
system just to use them.

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:25.110
Now you can surface those clips
directly within the application

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:28.620
that you want to
process the files with.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:33.159
And it makes it for a much more
efficient process, much faster.

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:35.280
And because we use natural
language processing,

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.672
you'll now find things that
you thought weren't there

00:08:37.672 --> 00:08:39.380
or you weren't asking
for them correctly,

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.465
so you were getting
incorrect responses.

00:08:41.465 --> 00:08:43.090
There's no more
Boolean search required

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:45.010
to find the value in the media.

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:48.700
So this idea of indexing
media is extremely important,

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.000
especially when you
have a lot of it.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.310
And the archive itself is
only as good as this index.

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:57.930
So we have many customers
who have many, many petabytes

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.910
of media out there,
and some of it

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:02.190
is multiple versions
of the same thing.

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:06.150
It could be localized versions
of a given title, for example,

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.910
all the different foreign
language versions of something.

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:10.350
But it's difficult
sometimes when

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:11.975
you have these massive
archives to know

00:09:11.975 --> 00:09:13.270
where there's duplication.

00:09:13.270 --> 00:09:16.170
So we have a lot of ways to
be able to detect duplication

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:19.770
and help call out some of
this derivative material

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:21.930
so that you can make the
footprint of your archives

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:25.990
much more manageable,
if you will.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:28.420
And then we also talk
about preservation storage.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:30.480
We have a number
of strategies we

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.400
talk about creating
preservation masters,

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.350
especially for
videotape, where we

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:37.085
can remove all of the
compression codecs

00:09:37.085 --> 00:09:38.460
for both the audio
and the video,

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:41.700
so we can have a true
archive representation

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.560
of that media that could
be read in the future.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.770
And then finally,
the third phase

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:48.930
of what we talk about
with archive migration

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:52.253
has to do with how we
leverage all of these assets.

00:09:52.253 --> 00:09:54.670
And so I'll talk a little bit
more about that in a second.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:57.580
So as I mentioned, we have all
these different types of media,

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.810
whether it's film or
videotape, audiotape, we

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:04.380
have lots of media on
data tape right now,

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:10.800
both LTO and T10K and some other
older formats like DLT, DAT.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:12.330
So there's media
all over the place.

00:10:12.330 --> 00:10:14.070
And so trying to
get that stuff off

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:16.980
of this legacy media,
which is non-archival,

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:19.890
to bring it into cloud, which is
archival, and make sense of it

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:22.920
is one of our key missions when
it comes to this digitization

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.420
phase.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:28.530
Then when it comes to
generating the assets' metadata,

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:32.040
we use the various tools we
have in our AI and ML toolset.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:35.437
As you can see here, we generate
the proxies, as I mentioned.

00:10:35.437 --> 00:10:36.270
We index that media.

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:38.130
And it's all fairly
automated, so when

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:39.900
you have a massive
collection you

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:43.140
can have a very substantial
database that can be searched

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:45.870
very quickly using natural
language processing to find

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:49.830
the material you're looking
for without really any hiccups.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:51.670
And then finally,
leveraging the content,

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:54.490
especially to get
this to the public.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:10:56.040
We find that the
metadata is actually

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.380
very useful in
direct-to-consumer plays

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:00.330
where people are looking
for a specific thing.

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:02.190
They may not be able to
remember the actor's name,

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:04.565
but they might remember the
character's name in the show.

00:11:04.565 --> 00:11:06.510
So all of this metadata
can travel along

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:09.007
with the media that would make
it easier for the consumers

00:11:09.007 --> 00:11:10.590
to find what they're
looking for, just

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:13.520
like the power of
a Google search.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:15.230
And then, of course,
our ecosystem

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:17.120
wouldn't be the
same if we didn't

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:19.140
have all these great
partners to deal with.

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:21.500
So we have all of these
wonderful content management

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:24.500
and archive migration partners
that we work with regularly

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:28.580
to help leverage the ways
we search this media,

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:31.220
the way we tag the media,
the way we store the media.

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:34.932
So we spend a lot of time
with our various customers

00:11:34.932 --> 00:11:36.890
and these partners to
really integrate and find

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:40.190
the best solutions for
folks to be able to manage

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:42.320
their massive media archives.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:45.020
Now, today we have
a very special guest

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:47.570
with Dan Lin from Viacom CBS.

00:11:47.570 --> 00:11:49.770
And they are
leveraging, at Viacom,

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:53.450
they're leveraging Google Cloud
Advanced Analytics to enhance

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:54.560
their content discovery.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:58.175
And they have lots of media
from lots of different sources.

00:11:58.175 --> 00:12:00.300
And so Dan is here to talk
a little bit about that.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:03.110
But I just want to point out
a couple of key things here.

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.000
Certainly with the
content recommendations,

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.550
there are over 4.2
million customers per day

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:09.590
looking for recommendations.

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:13.040
It's possible now to
provide actionable insights

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:15.410
for both marketing
and the ads teams

00:12:15.410 --> 00:12:19.100
to be able to analyze, in this
case, 2,500 pieces of content

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:19.820
daily.

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:22.730
And we deliver in-house
solutions for AI and ML,

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:26.120
which help leverage Google
Cloud products with just

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:27.320
a few developers in-house.

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:29.450
So leveraging the
power of AutoML

00:12:29.450 --> 00:12:31.610
and some of the other
partner tools we have,

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.280
people now have the power of
ML training their own models

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:35.960
without having to
be data scientists.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:38.210
So without further ado, I'd
like to introduce our very

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:39.923
special guest, Dan Lin.

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:46.380
DAN LIN: Thank you, Buzz,
for the introduction.

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:50.820
So I'm going to walk through
a little bit about one

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:53.160
of the projects that
we did with Google.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:56.910
It's part of a
much bigger journey

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:59.610
that Viacom CBS is undertaking
through our journey

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:01.410
to the cloud.

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:04.050
This project that we did
with Google is specifically

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:07.890
around Viacom's content.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:11.340
Viacom CBS has a lot
of different content,

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:14.250
both on the television and film.

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:16.900
In the case of film,
it's Paramount Pictures.

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:18.540
So this project that
we're working on

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:22.360
is specifically
around the Viacom.

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:25.560
So what we're trying
to do, I'm going

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:28.170
to walk you through a little
bit of project overview,

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:30.270
and talk a little bit
about the business

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:34.720
requirement and the architecture
and how we accomplish this.

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.720
So first, the project overview.

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:45.320
So we have roughly 17
petabytes of content,

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:47.700
and these content, like
what Buzz has mentioned,

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:50.610
is a lot of our source content.

00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:54.210
And where all the
derivative assets

00:13:54.210 --> 00:13:56.970
are generated to
for, ultimately,

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.810
for distribution and
packaging to multiple partners

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:05.140
and suppliers, and then
vendors and so forth.

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:09.330
So we call this the
content submission master.

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:12.630
So the idea of
this cloud journey

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:15.350
is to move all these
submission masters,

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:17.760
or at least make a copy of
it, and put it in the cloud

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:19.680
so that can be easily consumed.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:23.010
So it's a relatively early part
of the content supply chain.

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:29.220
And once we are able to put
all these content in the cloud,

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:35.010
then it'll be readily
available for transformation

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.030
or distribution, and
so on and so forth,

00:14:37.030 --> 00:14:41.197
and as well as all
other AI and ML things

00:14:41.197 --> 00:14:42.780
that we like to do
with these contents

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:47.560
that we weren't able
to do before on prem.

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:50.250
So let me talk a little bit
about the business requirement

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:52.240
for the cloud migration.

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:56.340
So the total amount
of assets we were

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.740
talking about for this
project was about 14 petabytes

00:14:58.740 --> 00:14:59.670
of content.

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:03.900
And we need to do this in
roughly a four-month period.

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:07.560
Besides moving a copy of
the content to the cloud,

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:12.180
we needed to make sure that the
data integrity is maintained.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:15.660
And there's really two
parts to the data integrity.

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:20.850
One is making sure
that the content that

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:23.310
while during the
transfer process

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:25.140
is not manipulated
and is identical

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:26.260
to the original source.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:29.200
So that's one aspect
of the integrity.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:33.380
The second aspect is that
the inventory is complete.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:33.880
Right.

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:35.710
So we did not miss anything.

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:38.140
So every single piece
of content that we're

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:43.920
supposed move to the cloud,
copy, made it to Google.

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:45.840
Lastly, if there's
any exceptions

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.430
as a part of that transfer,
we all, of course,

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:51.960
need to be able to easily
identify those and then

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:54.750
remediate them accordingly.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:57.840
So some of the key questions
that we needed to address

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:01.470
is, what's the best
way to move that data

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:03.300
in that short amount of time?

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:05.800
And there's a couple of
things that we looked at.

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:13.660
One was TSOP, which is basically
the Google Transfer tool.

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:18.990
Another method we looked
at was Transfer Appliance,

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:23.760
which is where they're shipping,
essentially, large hard drives,

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.875
and then we have to put
into the data center,

00:16:26.875 --> 00:16:30.480
move the data onto
these hard drives,

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:31.980
and then ship them
back to Google,

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:35.490
and then Google will suck
it into the Google Cloud.

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:37.680
And then the last
option is the gsutil,

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:41.015
which is a command line option.

00:16:41.015 --> 00:16:43.140
Other things that we have
to make decisions about--

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:45.440
where do we store it,
what tier do we store it?

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:47.660
Obviously, depending on the
tier and so on so forth,

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:50.580
and where we store it has
different cost implications.

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:52.720
Lastly is how do we
protect the data?

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:56.610
This data is the
source asset, so we

00:16:56.610 --> 00:17:00.510
need to make sure it does not
get unauthorized access or gets

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:02.160
tampered, and so on so forth.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:05.940
So how do we go about
protecting this data?

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:09.180
And some different types of
aspects of that data protection

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:12.329
is, do we turn on
things like versioning?

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:16.440
Do we end up using bucket lock
or object lock, [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:18.060
not choosing to do.

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:25.290
As well as who has access, and
that's basically the IAM roles.

00:17:25.290 --> 00:17:29.220
Lastly, from the
requirement point of view,

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.160
we also want to make sure
this can be done relatively

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:34.170
easily with minimum labor.

00:17:34.170 --> 00:17:38.150
Just because, again, Viacom CBS
is a very large content media

00:17:38.150 --> 00:17:38.650
company.

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:41.280
We have a lot of
content, and this is just

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:42.820
the first one of many.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:45.180
So we want to make sure that
this process and the tool

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:47.580
that we chose are efficient.

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:50.910
And another part
is that whatever

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:54.170
software we choose to
use to do this transfer

00:17:54.170 --> 00:17:57.630
and manage a transfer, and so
on so forth, as new requirements

00:17:57.630 --> 00:18:01.170
come up new features can
be added, can be deployed

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:02.230
and so on so forth.

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:04.740
And so that's where the
automated code deployment

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:07.050
comes in.

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:13.260
So, essentially, all these
data are on LTL tapes,

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:19.590
and it's actually being
supported by [? DBA. ?] So we

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:22.500
actually, basically, need
to pull all these data out

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:26.940
of [? DBA ?] from [INAUDIBLE],,
which was acquired by Oracle,

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:30.750
and I think lately it also
spun out to EcoDigital.

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:35.370
It's a fairly popular
[? MAM ?] system

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:38.310
that's used across many,
many media and entertainment

00:18:38.310 --> 00:18:39.410
companies.

00:18:39.410 --> 00:18:43.630
We're going to loaded it
onto a local SAN storage,

00:18:43.630 --> 00:18:45.720
and we're going to
use, essentially,

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:52.360
the TSOP software to eventually
move these data to Google.

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:54.450
So the primary
consideration on why

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:58.140
we chose TSOP was
basically that TSOP handles

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:02.700
a lot of the management
for us, especially in terms

00:19:02.700 --> 00:19:05.400
of validating, making
sure the data--

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:10.230
the integrity is maintained,
that is relatively easy

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:11.110
to operate.

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:13.500
There's not a lot
of people that need

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:17.040
to get involved in staging or
destaging equipment and what

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.410
have you, or loading
and unloading data

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:22.900
into physical equipment, in the
case of the Transfer Appliance.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:24.940
And so TSOP was
the obvious choice.

00:19:24.940 --> 00:19:28.510
It has a combination
of ease of use

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:33.610
and least amount of manual
intervention required.

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:39.280
And so that's why
we picked TSOP.

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:41.550
So beyond using
the TSOP, the TSOP

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:44.940
just basically handles
the data transfer.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:48.780
I'm going to talk a little
bit about those architecture.

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:52.100
What's important is that when
we're moving all this data out

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:54.540
of [? DBA, ?] obviously,
is carry over some

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:59.503
of the proprietary things that's
specific to [? DBA. ?] Part

00:19:59.503 --> 00:20:01.920
of what we want to do in the
process is not carry any sort

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:03.640
of technical debt.

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:06.120
So information that's very
specific to [? DBA, ?]

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:08.960
for example, how objects are
referenced and so on and so

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:09.460
forth.

00:20:09.460 --> 00:20:12.600
We want to change
the architecture such

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:17.760
that we're using for a
more native GUIs that

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:19.980
more conforms to
modern architecture.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:23.718
So not only do we do
the data transfer,

00:20:23.718 --> 00:20:25.260
we actually did a
lot of manipulation

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:27.390
in terms of file names
and so on so forth.

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:29.910
So that by the time
it gets to Google

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:35.340
it doesn't have any of the
historical information that's

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:36.930
specific to [? DBA. ?]

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:40.220
So one of things that happened,
you'll see this workflow,

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:43.980
I won't cover all of it, but
you'll see all the other things

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:48.840
that we did by leveraging
things like BigQuery

00:20:48.840 --> 00:20:52.800
and some custom stuff that's
written to basically do

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:56.130
all these manipulation
and processing.

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.110
[INAUDIBLE] with
TSOP was how we were

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:05.060
able to manage the whole
data migration to Google.

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:08.180
So with that, this
is the conclusion

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:09.650
of this particular project.

00:21:09.650 --> 00:21:12.542
We were able to achieve
this in four months.

00:21:12.542 --> 00:21:14.000
One of the key
reasons that I think

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:16.328
is worth pointing out
why we chose Google out

00:21:16.328 --> 00:21:18.620
of all the other vendors,
that there are two key things

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:21.920
that we thought that set
the Google, especially

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:24.140
the S3 storage, apart.

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:25.580
One, is the fact
that, regardless

00:21:25.580 --> 00:21:29.120
of the tier or storage, the
access is always instantaneous.

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:30.750
Whereas, some of
the other providers,

00:21:30.750 --> 00:21:32.570
you store it on
coder tier and you

00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:35.240
need that piece of
information, it's

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:37.520
not necessarily instantaneous.

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:41.750
The second thing that we
thought made Google stand out

00:21:41.750 --> 00:21:44.990
in this use case was
the fact that there

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:51.570
is a special continental bucket
that we were able to store to.

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:54.560
So in a scenario where we
have assets that we need

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:57.190
protected and have multiple
copies, for example.

00:21:57.190 --> 00:22:01.580
So that's not subject
to localized failures.

00:22:01.580 --> 00:22:04.910
We do not necessarily need to
make full copies, for example,

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:08.075
to availability zones or
regions or what have you.

00:22:08.075 --> 00:22:11.390
There's a concept of
a continental region.

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:15.380
So, effectively, that will allow
us to achieve that requirement,

00:22:15.380 --> 00:22:18.800
but without having to pay
double to copy, essentially,

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:19.970
with another provider.

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:22.250
So those are actually
the key reasons

00:22:22.250 --> 00:22:26.260
why we picked Google
for this project.

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:30.540
So in summary, we were able
to achieve this in four months

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:34.020
with a lot of help from Google,
it was a great partnership.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:38.820
This is the first of many
content library migrations,

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:41.550
and as we speak,
we are now moving

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:45.437
on to the next one, which
is Paramount Pictures.

00:22:45.437 --> 00:22:46.520
BUZZ HAYS: Thank you, Dan.

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:48.980
That's great, and it's
a very nice description

00:22:48.980 --> 00:22:51.170
of how you did this.

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:53.210
As you can see, this
is representative

00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:56.390
of how Viacom CBS is managing
their massive archives.

00:22:56.390 --> 00:22:57.980
But, Dan, in your
experience, you

00:22:57.980 --> 00:22:59.010
pointed out a couple
of things here

00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:00.635
I wanted to ask you
a little bit about.

00:23:00.635 --> 00:23:03.510
It's the integrity checks,
the checksums, part of this.

00:23:03.510 --> 00:23:06.000
It's an extremely important
part of any of these archives.

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:07.820
And if you talk
to any archivist,

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:11.150
whether they refer to it
as a checksum, an MD5 hash

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:13.370
or [INAUDIBLE],,
everyone is concerned

00:23:13.370 --> 00:23:16.730
about checking the
file integrity of what

00:23:16.730 --> 00:23:19.280
those files are as
they're stored in a cloud.

00:23:19.280 --> 00:23:21.895
So could you talk a little
bit about that process?

00:23:21.895 --> 00:23:23.270
Is that a fully
automated process

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:25.040
that's part of your
[? MAM, ?] or how do

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:28.790
the checksums happen over time?

00:23:28.790 --> 00:23:30.808
DAN LIN: Yes, it definitely is.

00:23:30.808 --> 00:23:33.350
So the way we do it today, like
you said, a lot of this stuff

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:38.480
is stored on LTL, so LTL has
the lifecycle, essentially,

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:39.030
if you will.

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:42.980
So we have a business
requirement where every year

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:47.960
we're actually pulling
back every piece of asset

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:52.160
and rehashing, basically,
read the content into memory

00:23:52.160 --> 00:23:56.990
and comparing the MD5
against the original source.

00:23:56.990 --> 00:23:59.300
And that's how we make
sure nothing got changed.

00:23:59.300 --> 00:24:01.383
And, obviously, they're
saying that doesn't match,

00:24:01.383 --> 00:24:03.375
and new copies get created.

00:24:03.375 --> 00:24:05.250
And most of the time
when things don't match,

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:08.720
it's due to bit rot
or what have you.

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:11.060
So it definitely does
occur, especially

00:24:11.060 --> 00:24:13.312
given the amount of
content that we have.

00:24:13.312 --> 00:24:15.770
Obviously, in the grand scheme
of the population of content

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:18.640
we have, the amount of bit rot
is actually very, very small,

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:21.830
but we want to make sure
we're catching those

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:26.580
so that we always have
confidence in our content.

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:30.010
Especially on the film side,
which the content sometimes

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:32.750
maybe can take hundreds of
millions of dollars to make.

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:36.110
We definitely want to make
sure those contents are not

00:24:36.110 --> 00:24:38.700
contaminated in any fashion.

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:40.450
BUZZ HAYS: Yeah, that's
a very good point,

00:24:40.450 --> 00:24:43.410
because a lot of the archived
media that's been used to date

00:24:43.410 --> 00:24:44.970
is somewhat fragile, right?

00:24:44.970 --> 00:24:47.490
And so I imagine
prior to this you

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:50.470
had to have a migration strategy
to get from, for example,

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:52.500
one LTL format to
the next one, just

00:24:52.500 --> 00:24:54.810
to be able to preserve the
archive at some cadence.

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:57.990
But now, once you've
moved things to cloud,

00:24:57.990 --> 00:24:59.970
I think that you can
breathe a sigh of relief

00:24:59.970 --> 00:25:02.250
that you don't have to do
those migrations anymore.

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:03.870
But this also speaks
to one question

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:07.560
I had is about with a
company as big as Viacom CBS,

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:10.860
you're dealing with a number of
different libraries or archives

00:25:10.860 --> 00:25:12.240
from different entities.

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:16.950
And it seems that those
can be somewhat uniform

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:18.960
within the company, even
though the assets came

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:20.100
from disparate locations.

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:22.650
They probably came with
all manner of metadata--

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:24.270
or no metadata, who knows.

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:26.820
But are you seeing
this as an opportunity

00:25:26.820 --> 00:25:29.820
to create a uniform database
of all of this media

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:32.480
that you now have
within Viacom CBS?

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:34.230
DAN LIN: Yeah, that's
definitely the idea.

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:35.970
I mean, it runs the gamut.

00:25:35.970 --> 00:25:37.890
I mean, typically,
the amount of metadata

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.230
that we have and
maintain it's really

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:41.940
a function of multiple things.

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:44.157
But one thing that it
definitely very much aligned to

00:25:44.157 --> 00:25:45.240
is the value of the asset.

00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:46.950
Typically, the more
valuable the asset,

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:50.070
the more effort was put into
maintaining the metadata

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:54.580
and cataloging in the content.

00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:58.050
We also have content
that's prefinished work

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:00.780
as part of production
process, or what have you.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:03.248
Especially 10 years
ago, 20 years ago,

00:26:03.248 --> 00:26:05.040
again, not having the
digital, so obviously

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:07.330
the level of catalog varies.

00:26:07.330 --> 00:26:10.740
So as we migrate more and
more stuff to the cloud

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:12.720
and we're leveraging
things like AI and ML

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:15.690
to do things like automated
video and audio conformance

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:18.730
and what have you, there's
also a lot of opportunity

00:26:18.730 --> 00:26:21.670
to do auto metadata
tagging and so forth.

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:23.970
Again, a lot of
our legacy content

00:26:23.970 --> 00:26:27.822
that maybe doesn't have the same
amount of richness in metadata.

00:26:27.822 --> 00:26:29.280
And as we do more
and more of this,

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:31.655
we will hopefully
be able to monetize.

00:26:31.655 --> 00:26:33.030
I didn't find new
[INAUDIBLE] how

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:35.100
to use, especially
content that we

00:26:35.100 --> 00:26:36.943
haven't touched in a long time.

00:26:36.943 --> 00:26:37.610
BUZZ HAYS: Yeah.

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:39.410
And to that point,
final question,

00:26:39.410 --> 00:26:42.860
is you mentioned the notion of
the different tiers of storage

00:26:42.860 --> 00:26:45.530
we have at Google,
and how your retrieval

00:26:45.530 --> 00:26:49.580
times are the same time to
first byte on each tier.

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:52.550
In my experience, I'm noticing
that a lot of companies have--

00:26:52.550 --> 00:26:54.480
their archive is
not a static thing,

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:56.720
it's an actual living
breathing thing,

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:59.240
and you're constantly
finding ways

00:26:59.240 --> 00:27:00.802
to monetize what's in there.

00:27:00.802 --> 00:27:03.260
Is that part of the strategy
at this point with Viacom CBS,

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:05.750
is to start leveraging some
of the older content that's

00:27:05.750 --> 00:27:08.570
been sitting in the archive that
may not have been as visible

00:27:08.570 --> 00:27:11.027
or as searchable in the past?

00:27:11.027 --> 00:27:11.610
DAN LIN: Yeah.

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:13.400
I mean, it really
runs the gamut.

00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:19.282
I mean, again, we have things
that are like the raw--

00:27:19.282 --> 00:27:21.115
and on the film side
and the television side

00:27:21.115 --> 00:27:21.980
it's actually quite different.

00:27:21.980 --> 00:27:23.480
On the film side
we have things that

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:25.850
are, for example, preservation
master, which were

00:27:25.850 --> 00:27:28.430
the original raw DPX files.

00:27:28.430 --> 00:27:31.160
We probably won't be
necessarily working

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:33.190
a lot against
those type of files

00:27:33.190 --> 00:27:35.780
per se, right, in that space.

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:39.820
We will probably use
like a mezzanine format.

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:44.600
So legacy sort of
mezzanine sort of content

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:47.990
that we might do the things
that you're talking about.

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:49.480
But who knows, right?

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:52.115
In five years or
10 years, the thing

00:27:52.115 --> 00:27:53.615
that seems to be
relatively constant

00:27:53.615 --> 00:27:56.180
is that the quality of
the content that people

00:27:56.180 --> 00:27:59.300
want to seem to work again just
keeps going higher and higher.

00:27:59.300 --> 00:28:02.150
So maybe somewhere
down the road we

00:28:02.150 --> 00:28:05.000
might be working directly
against the original

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:06.730
preservation masters.

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:07.730
BUZZ HAYS: That's great.

00:28:07.730 --> 00:28:09.772
Well, Dan, thank you,
again, for your time today.

00:28:09.772 --> 00:28:11.260
Thank you for
contributing to this,

00:28:11.260 --> 00:28:12.900
and thank you for
working with us.

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:14.317
This has been such
a great project

00:28:14.317 --> 00:28:16.340
and we look forward
to many, many more.

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:19.880
And thank you all
for listening, and I

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:22.100
hope you found this
useful and entertaining.

00:28:22.100 --> 00:28:26.790
And expect more to come from
Viacom CBS and Google Cloud.

